  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nOur major pattern shift begins today as low pressure begins to\ndrops across western Canada. This will produce gusty winds\nacross a good chunk of eastern Idaho. Gusts in the 25-35 mph are\nexpected, with some gusts closer to 40 mph across the central\nmountains. We should also see some mid level clouds developing\nthe area this afternoon. The concern isn't necessarily rain, as\nit should be way too dry to get measurable rain at least\ninitially. The concern is sprinkles/virga and enhance wind\ngusts. There is a 10-50% chance for gusts over 35 mph for late\nafternoon into the evening hours. While the chance is extremely\nlow, we might even see a couple of thunderstorms. Overnight, we\ndo expect a slow uptick in the potential for trace rainfall\namounts or may a couple of hundredths worth of rain, mainly\nacross higher elevations. Temperatures will actually be a bit\nwarmer today especially where we might get a little bit of help\nfrom downslope. That said, it still will be in the mid 70s to\nmid 80s for the valley. We can say goodbye to those temperatures\nfor a bit after today. \n\nShowers and storms will begin to increase tomorrow fairly\nquickly once we get later in the day. By tomorrow night and\nlasting into Monday, more widespread rain and mountain snow is\nlikely with some embedded thunderstorms. The pattern, although\nshowing some minor shifts across the western U.S. still develops\na strong closed low across California, as the flow splits. The\nnorthern end of the split maintains a slow moving through\nextending through western and central Idaho and north into\nCanada. We are staying on the warm side of the storm through\nMonday. This is helping keep mountain snowfall potential north\nacross Montana except for the trough/area of low pressure across\ncentral Idaho and around Island Park. The pattern also holds\ntaking the remnants of Rachel all the way north into Utah,\nWyoming and Idaho. We continue to refine and increase\nprecipitation amounts, as it does appear there is still a shift\nto the west with heavier precipitation. The latest forecast has\nmore widespread amounts of 1-2\" now back across the Snake Plain\nand into the central mountains from the Wood River Valley\nthrough the southeast corner of Lemhi County. There is still\nsome expectation we could see totals above 2\" along and east of\nI-15. Even where we may not see 1\"+ precipitation amounts \nacross the central mountains, totals are still running \n0.40-0.90\". If you look at the higher end of the Blend of Model \nrainfall totals, it is showing widespread 1.5-3.0\"...highest \nacross the same strip of the central mountains as well as the \neastern and southeast highlands. The absolute highest in the \nBlend is coming in at 2.0-3.5\" and locally higher near 4\" in a \nfew spots. While the absolute highest have a very low chance of \noccurring, some areas seeing amounts of 2-3\" is definitely out \nof the question.Based on the current trends accumulating snow is\nconfined to mostly above 8000ft now. Light amounts are still \nexpected, but highest peak and ridgelines may indeed several \ninches of wet snow accumulation across the central mountains and\nalong the Montana border. \n\nLow pressure remains across the region through midweek, so do\nexpect show showers to stick around along with those cooler\ntemperatures. Most versions of the GFS and ECMWF (including\nensembles) show high pressure redeveloping across the West later\nnext week.",
